    (v_topic_id, 'A 24-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for bilateral clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Varus", "Equinus", "Cavus", "Supination", "Adductus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-week-old infant, born breech, presents for a routine well-child check. Examination reveals asymmetric thigh skin folds and a positive Ortolani maneuver on the left. What is the most appropriate initial imaging modality to confirm the diagnosis?', '["Ultrasound of the hips", "Bone scan", "AP radiograph of the pelvis", "MRI of the hips", "CT scan of the pelvis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is most accurately assessed with ultrasound in infants under 4-6 months of age because the femoral head and acetabulum are predominantly cartilaginous and not fully visible on radiographs. A positive Ortolani test (reduction of a dislocated hip) strongly suggests DDH.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #14698: An 11-year-old overweight adolescent undergoes in situ pinning for a stable Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE). At follow-up, the patient complains of severe stiffness and loss of joint space is noted on X-ray. What is this devastating complication?', '["Avascular necrosis of the femoral head", "Chondrolysis", "Nonunion", "Heterotopic ossification", "Infection"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 10-year-old falls from monkey bars and sustains a significantly displaced extension-type supracondylar humerus fracture. Upon examination, the child is unable to make an ''OK'' sign (unable to flex the IP joint of the thumb and DIP joint of the index finger). Which nerve is most likely injured?', '["Ulnar nerve", "Anterior interosseous nerve (AIN)", "Axillary nerve", "Radial nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The anterior interosseous nerve (AIN), a branch of the median nerve, is the most commonly injured nerve in extension-type supracondylar humerus fractures. Injury manifests as an inability to flex the IP joint of the thumb (flexor pollicis longus) and the DIP of the index finger (flexor digitorum profundus), resulting in an inability to make the ''''A-OK'''' sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 10-year-old falls from a trampoline and sustains a significantly displaced extension-type supracondylar humerus fracture. Upon examination, the child is unable to make an ''OK'' sign (unable to flex the IP joint of the thumb and DIP joint of the index finger). Which nerve is most likely injured?', '["Anterior interosseous nerve (AIN)", "Musculocutaneous nerve", "Axillary nerve", "Ulnar nerve", "Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The anterior interosseous nerve (AIN), a branch of the median nerve, is the most commonly injured nerve in extension-type supracondylar humerus fractures. Injury manifests as an inability to flex the IP joint of the thumb (flexor pollicis longus) and the DIP of the index finger (flexor digitorum profundus), resulting in an inability to make the ''''A-OK'''' sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old female football lineman complains of insidious onset, mechanical low back pain that is exacerbated by extension activities (e.g., back walkovers). Neurologic exam is normal. Oblique radiographs of the lumbar spine demonstrate a ''collar'' on the ''Scotty dog''. What is the specific anatomic defect in this condition?', '["Fracture of the transverse process", "Defect in the pars interarticularis", "Herniation of the nucleus pulposus", "Congenital failure of segmentation of the lumbar vertebrae", "Defect in the vertebral body endplate (Schmorl node)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Spondylolysis is a stress fracture or defect of the pars interarticularis. It is highly prevalent in adolescent athletes participating in sports involving repetitive lumbar hyperextension. On oblique radiographs, a radiolucent gap in the pars resembles a collar on the neck of the ''''Scotty dog''''.');
